    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite (the single `AcceptanceTest`) builds and passes. \n\nThe implementation is complete. Here's a summary of what I did to satisfy the spec \u2014 *\"A new owner's membershipLevel cannot exceed one above the current maximum membershipLevel among their household members; return the capped membershipLevel. With no existing household member no cap applies.\"*\n\n**`Owner.java` (model)**\n- Added a persisted `membershipLevelCap` field (`@Column(name = \"membership_level_cap\")`) with accessors \u2014 this stores the ceiling computed at creation so it survives the round trip and is applied on every `GET`.\n- Added static `cappedMembershipLevel(int level, Integer cap)` \u2014 the single definition of the ceiling: returns the level unchanged when `cap` is `null`, otherwise `Math.min(level, cap)`.\n- `getMembershipLevel()` now returns the points-derived level held to that ceiling.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (create path)**\n- Added `householdMembershipLevelCap(householdId)`: over the existing, non-deleted household members (computed before the new owner is saved, so it excludes the new owner), it stamps each member's household count, takes the max `membershipLevel`, and returns `max + 1`; returns `null` when the household has no existing members (no cap).\n- `createOwner` sets this cap on the owner right before persisting.\n\n**Schema** \u2014 added the `membership_level_cap` column to all four schemas (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres); tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column had to be declared explicitly.\n\nThe capped `membershipLevel` flows out through the existing MapStruct mapping (property-name match) with no mapper change needed, and the OpenAPI `membershipLevel` field already bounds the value at `1..4`.",
